  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nMorning Fog:\n\nFoggy conditions expected this morning as a ridge builds into the \nUpper Midwest. Light northeast winds coupled with a moist\nairmass of dewpoints near 70F and a stabilizing atmosphere will\nall aid in fog development. The fog may be dense at times with \nthe 00Z HREF suggesting a 20 to 40% chance for visibilities to \ndrop below 1/4 mile. Any fog or low stratus that develops will \nlikely dissipate by mid-morning. \n\nShowers/Storms Return Sunday:\n\nDry conditions will be with us through this evening until a \nwave moves down from northern Minnesota late tonight into \nSunday. Several CAMs show showers and storms sparking off this\nwave from eastern SD to central Minnesota early Sunday morning.\nShowers and storms continue down into northern Iowa through \nSunday afternoon and evening. Some of these could get into \nportions of northeast Iowa and southeast Minnesota (20 to 40%). \nAny strong to severe storm chances should stay off to our west \nas local area shear remains quite weak. Additionally, as the \nridge continues to build northward, low level dry air will be \nin place resulting in a cap across much of forecast area. \n\nShowers/Storm Potential Persists Monday:\n\nAnother wave moves northeastward into northern Minnesota and \nfar northwestern Wisconsin into Monday, potentially resulting \nin showers and storms for some of our northwestern counties (15\nto 25%). Despite the ridge building in, temperatures will only \nbe slightly above normal as surface winds will be out of the \nsoutheast. \n\nAdditional Shower/Storm Chances Into Midweek:\n\nThe upper level pattern shifts to zonal/southwest flow due to \nan approaching shortwave trough heading into Tuesday. All three\nof the GFS, ECMWF, and CMC have this trough and associated cold\nfront passing through late Tuesday into Wednesday. There are \nsome differences in the timing of the front with these models\nand can expect these details to get ironed out as forecast hour\nnears. Moisture transport increases early Tuesday ahead of the \nfront which gives the region its first dose of precipitation \nchances as the front eventually moves through late Tuesday into\nWednesday where the latest NBM has 50 to 80% PoPs with the \nfrontal passage. \n\nCurrent uncertainty on coverage of convection is due to\npotential low level dry air that storms would have to overcome.\nTherefore, lower PoPs may be realized during this timeframe. \nA low chance for severe weather is expected with frontal \npassage due to 35 to 45 kts of effective shear and 500 to 1250 \nJ/kg of MLCAPE. Most AI and ML guidance have a low chance (5 to \n15%) for any kind of severe weather to occur. With a shift \ntowards more southwesterly flow in the low levels, temperatures \nincrease back up into the mid to upper 80s for Tuesday before \nfalling back down into the mid 70s to mid 80s for Wednesday.",
